---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How this site is built"
  description="Astro, Azure Static Web Apps, Bicep and GitHub Actions — the whole pipeline, including the bits that were more awkward than the docs suggest."
>
  <h1>How this site is built</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Astro, static, on Azure Static Web Apps. No server, no adapter, and exactly one script — which started as zero, and the story of how it got to one is most of this page.
  </p>

  <h2>The stack, briefly</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Choice</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Site generator</td><td>Astro 7, static output</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Content</td><td>MDX in content collections, Zod-typed frontmatter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Styling</td><td>One hand-written stylesheet, CSS custom properties</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Type</td><td>Fraunces, self-hosted, for headings; system stacks below</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Azure Static Web Apps, Free tier</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Bicep, one resource</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pipeline</td><td>GitHub Actions, with PR preview environments</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Content is typed, not trusted</h2>
  <p>
    Both collections — <code>posts</code> and <code>projects</code> — share a Zod schema for title, description, date, tags and draft status, with projects adding a status enum and
    optional repo/live links.
    A typo in front matter means I won't accidentally post a half-completed thought or, worse, a syntax error <i>gasp</i>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drafts are visible in <code>astro dev</code> and excluded from a production build.
  </p>

  <h2>One script, and what it cost</h2>
  <p>
    This site started with a guideline (Claude says it was a rule): no client-side JavaScript on a content page.
    Dark mode followed <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> and that was the end of it.
    The rule held until the first person asked for a theme toggle. That person was me, after looking at the boring UI I had just built.
  </p>
  <p>
    So there is exactly one script. It sets a <code>data-theme</code> attribute before <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> parses, reads and writes one <code>localStorage</code> key, and falls back to <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> when it doesn't run.
    About thirty lines, no dependencies, no framework — the type of efficiency that software dev promises but rarely achieves.
  </p>
  <p>It wasn't free, and the bill is the interesting part:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The <abbr title="Content Security Policy">CSP</abbr> pins it by hash.</strong>
      Having co-authored a few internal docs on ISO standards over the years, this was the first thing to set my cyber senses tingling.
      The boring technical bit: <code>script-src</code> carries a sha256 of that exact script rather than <code>'self'</code>, so nothing else can execute (cybersecurity win!).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every themed rule now has two routes.</strong> The OS preference when the reader hasn't chosen (dark for those L337 hackers and light for everyone else), <code>[data-theme]</code> when they have.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Syntax highlighting is still build-time.</strong> Shiki emits both themes as CSS custom properties and the switch picks one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    It's always great to design the best and coolest thingy, but if no one can see it or read it or touch it — then it's really just décor.
    So I made it easy: <strong>one Bicep file</strong> (keep it KISS and DRY!).
    The pipeline owns the deployment; Bicep owns the resource.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline, and the Oryx detour</h2>
  <p>
    The default Static Web Apps workflow hands your repository to Oryx, Azure's build engine (so thoughtful!), and lets it work out how to build the site.
    That's convenient right up until you want any customisation or to run something completely different like Astro or Docusaurus.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Sometimes the absence of something is all you need".
    GitHub Actions checks out, installs on a pinned Node, runs <code>astro check</code>, runs <code>astro build</code>, and then the deploy action runs with <code>skip_app_build: true</code> — it uploads <code>dist</code> and nothing else.
  </p>

  <Callout type="tip" title="The gotcha">
    With <code>skip_app_build</code>, <code>app_location</code> stops meaning "where the
    source is" and starts meaning "where the built output is". And because only
    <code>dist</code> gets uploaded, <code>staticwebapp.config.json</code> has to be inside
    it — so it lives in <code>public/</code> and Astro copies it across. Leave it at the
    repository root and it's silently ignored: no error, no headers, just a site that scores
    badly on a security scan for no visible reason.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Preview before publishing to GA — not doing that is like forgetting to put pants on, and that just isn't comfortable for anyone.
    Pull requests get their own preview environment on a stable URL, and a second job tears it down when the PR closes (<span role="img" aria-label="celebrate">🎉</span> yay for automation).
  </p>

  <h2>Headers and caching</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cache all the things!*</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    <small>
      *: <code>staticwebapp.config.json</code> sets a restrictive CSP, HSTS, <code>nosniff</code>, a referrer policy and a <code>Permissions-Policy</code> that switches off hardware APIs this site shouldn't be asking for.
      Astro's hashed assets under <code>/_astro/</code> get a year of immutable caching; HTML gets <code>must-revalidate</code>, so a deploy is visible immediately.
    </small>
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the confession. The CSP isn't as tight as I'd like — <code>style-src</code> still carries <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>, and there are two culprits.
    Astro inlines its smaller stylesheets straight into the page, and Shiki writes every syntax colour as an inline <code>style</code> attribute.
    The first I could fix. The second would mean <code>style-src-attr</code>, which Firefox and Safari don't implement — so my code blocks would go grey on two engines out of three (a cybersecurity loss, but a readability win).
  </p>
  <p>
    So it stays. And I'd rather say it here than have you find it in View Source, which — let's be honest — you were going to do anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>What's deliberately missing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics.</li>
    <li>No comments (we've got enough of those elsewhere).</li>
    <li>
      No third-party requests. KISS!
    </li>
    <li>
      No interactive islands, yet! That's very much a <i>"let's build it when it's needed"</i> — a completely valid engineering decision.
    </li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
